# Accessibility and performance gates

- Use semantic HTML first. Add ARIA only when native semantics are insufficient.
- All interactive elements must be reachable and operable with a keyboard, show a visible focus state, and have an accessible name.
- Preserve heading order, form labels, error association, landmark structure, and meaningful alternative text.
- Do not encode meaning by color alone. Verify contrast in every state, including disabled, hover, focus, and text over imagery.
- Touch targets must be comfortably usable. Hover-only information needs a keyboard/touch equivalent.
- Reserve image/media dimensions to reduce layout shift. Load only the assets needed for the initial viewport.
- Avoid unnecessary client-side JavaScript and large animation dependencies on static pages.
- Check the browser console and failed network requests. A visually attractive page with runtime errors does not pass.
- Test at minimum around 375px, 768px, and 1440px viewport widths, plus any product-specific breakpoint.
